  "intro": "Fifth graders read multisyllabic words by using letter-sound patterns, syllable rules, and word parts (roots, prefixes, suffixes). Knowing Greek and Latin roots helps unlock meaning: 'spect' means look (inspect), 'struct' means build (construct), 'port' means carry (transport).",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "The word 'inspect' has the root 'spect' (look). What does 'inspect' mean?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "'In-' means into; 'spect' means look.",
          "So 'inspect' means to look into/examine closely."
        ],
        "answer": "To look closely at / examine."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Break 'unbelievable' into prefix, root, and suffix.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Prefix 'un-' (not); root 'believe'; suffix '-able' (able to be).",
          "So un + believe + able."
        ],
        "answer": "un + believe + able."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "How many syllables are in 'opportunity'? (op-por-tu-ni-ty)",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "I clap: op-por-tu-ni-ty.",
          "That is five parts.",
          "So it has five syllables."
        ],
        "answer": "Five."
